From: dasher@netcom.com (Anton Sherwood)
Date: Sat, 23 Apr 1994 15:52:07 -0700
Subject: [#94-4-184] the evolution of death

The acute Eric Forste writes:
> As far as reproductive success of *all* of your genes is
> concerned, on average, any two of your kids is as good as you.
> The upshot of this is that any gene that kills you, but doesn't
> kill you until after you've raised three or more kids to
> breeding age, will simply *not* be selected against.

This goes not far enough.  The young and the old have different 
interests--the old lion has a harem which the young lion would 
like to take over, for example.  A gene which favors three 
children over their parent not only resists being weeded out, 
it is favored.

