From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Sun, 24 Apr 94 4:31:06 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-189] immortality

James Baker writes: 
>
>Single-celled creatures don't age and die as we do but multi-celled
>creatures age and die by design. I think I know the reason why.  
>
>First a little background. 
>
>With single celled creatures two strains from the same parent-cell could
>each mutate into better adapted creatures yet there is no way these two
>advantageous adaptations could fuse in one organism. Sexual reproduction,

I believe that there are at least a couple of ways for single-celled
creatures to share genes--if I'm not mistaken, paramecia conjugate
(occasionally exchange genes, even though their usual method of
reproduction is by splitting), and bacteria have some way of passing
genes to each other--there's a theory that resistance to antibiotics
can spread this way. (Both of these are based on somewhat vague
memories--I hope that someone here has enough background in biology
to confirm or deny them.)

>with two parents allows advantageous adaptations from different strains to
>combine in one organism. Evolution took a leap forward with the creation of
>sexually reproduced multi-celled creatures. In effect the whole gene pool
>is working simultaneously on adaptations for the next generation. (Single
>celled creatures by comparison are only working on adaptations for their
>single line of descendants.) 
>
>The sexually reproduced organisms have an advantage in terms of rapidly
>adapting the whole gene pool to changing conditions, but there is a
>problem. The older organisms compete with the current generation for
>resources. The older organisms often win by virtue of their size or just by
>random selection and their presence in the ecological niche. What's needed
>to make the system work is a way of promoting turnover; an unfair advantage
>for the latest generation. 
>
I don't think that this is a serious problem--some species do have
direct competition between older and younger members--komodo dragons
would eat their young if the adults weren't too big to climb trees....
The answer in the case of the komodo dragons is that the older and
younger members of the species aren't exactly in the same niche.

I think that your argument implies that the younger generation
would necessarily have a genetic advantage which must be artificially
protected (beyond the obvious need that the young have to be supported
until they can take care of themselves)--but if they have a genetic
advantage, then they shouldn't need extra help.

My impression is that there are a few multi-cellular species (the
only one I can think of is carp) that don't age, and they're doing
ok. Presumably, there are enough dangers for adult carp that niches
for young ones keep opening up.

My guess is that ageing is either a glitch or a side effect of
something useful (possibly cancer prevention).

>So nature invents a biological clock to automatically kill the older
>multi-celled organisms. They die automatically at some age safely beyond
>their reproductive stage to make room in the niche for their young. 

This sounds like a theory that could be tested with some sort of
artificial life---does anyone know of any simulations?

>[parts deleted] 
>
>An interesting area to study this possibility is human egg cells. My
>understanding of human reproduction says that a woman's egg cells are
>formed in utero. Some 20 - 40 years later they potentially become
>fertilized and become babies. 
>
>Yet a 20 year old mother and a 40 year old mother both bear infants. (Forty
>year old mothers don't bear little 20-year-olds.) This implies that the
>human aging clock is started after fertilization and follows a predictable
>course. But human egg cells age differently or not at all. 

Actually, the risk of birth defects does go up as the mother gets
older.

